Subject: [PATCH 1/8] arith_dec: ixheaac_mult64_sat() without 64-bit division

VibeSDR patch for libxaac v0.1.14 (commit 2fbadd57; the decoder VibeServer links for xHE-AAC). Apache-2.0, as libxaac.
Written for VibeSDR, 2026-10-29; applied in the order of xaac-patches/series by vibeserver/linux/build-libxaac.sh (Linux
and Mac). Every file it changes carries a "Modified by VibeSDR" notice (Apache-2.0 section 4(b)). Measured with
xaacbench on the 7 ISO conformance streams, 12 xHE test streams (usac/xhe) and 4 off-air DAB+ unit logs, at PCM16 as
the server decodes, on arm64, armhf, ARMv6, i386 and amd64 (qemu) and the Mac. Instruction counts: qemu, guest
instructions per second of audio.

USAC spectral dequantisation (ixheaacd_esc_iquant) calls ixheaac_mult64_sat() once per coefficient. Its overflow test
was four 64-bit divisions; on 32-bit ARM that is libgcc's __udivmoddi4, 14 % of all instructions on an FD stream on the
Pi 2 build. The compiler's checked multiply gives the same saturation without dividing. Output bit-identical on every
stream and arch. (Measured together with 0003: armhf Fd_2_c1_0x05 33.7 -> 25.0 M instr/s.)

diff --git a/common/ixheaac_basic_ops40.h b/common/ixheaac_basic_ops40.h
index afd3a86..4a57190 100644
--- a/common/ixheaac_basic_ops40.h
+++ b/common/ixheaac_basic_ops40.h
@@ -16,6 +16,10 @@
  *
  *****************************************************************************
  * Originally developed and contributed by Ittiam Systems Pvt. Ltd, Bangalore
+ *
+ * Modified by VibeSDR, 2026-10-29: ixheaac_mult64_sat() detects overflow with the
+ * compiler's checked multiply (GCC, clang) instead of four 64-bit divisions, a
+ * library call per coefficient on 32-bit targets. Same results.
 */
 #ifndef IXHEAAC_BASIC_OPS40_H
 #define IXHEAAC_BASIC_OPS40_H
@@ -174,6 +178,12 @@ static PLATFORM_INLINE WORD64 ixheaac_mult64(WORD32 a, WORD32 b) {
 static PLATFORM_INLINE WORD64 ixheaac_mult64_sat(WORD64 a, WORD64 b) {
   WORD64 result;
 
+#if defined(__GNUC__) || defined(__clang__)
+  /* VibeSDR: the same saturation, without dividing: on overflow the product's
+     sign is the operands' sign (neither can be 0). */
+  if (__builtin_mul_overflow(a, b, &result)) return ((a < 0) != (b < 0)) ? MIN_64 : MAX_64;
+  return result;
+#endif
   if (a > 0 && b > 0 && a > MAX_64 / b) return MAX_64;
   if (a < 0 && b > 0 && a < MIN_64 / b) return MIN_64;
   if (a > 0 && b < 0 && b < MIN_64 / a) return MIN_64;
